          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nErnest Hemingway was born on July 21, 1899, in Oak Park, Illinois. He was raised in a suburb of Chicago but spent much of his time in northern Michigan, where he learned to fish and hunt. These experiences in nature influenced many of his later writings. Hemingway's early education was at Oak Park and River Forest High School, where he played sports and wrote for the school newspaper.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War I Service</strong><br>\nAt the outbreak of World War I, Hemingway volunteered for ambulance service in Italy. He was severely wounded on the Austro-Italian front and spent a significant time recuperating at a hospital in Milan. This period was crucial in shaping Hemingway's worldview and his writing style, which would later be characterized by a focus on courage and endurance in the face of tragedy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Writing Career Beginnings</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Hemingway worked as a journalist before becoming a full-time writer. He moved to Paris in the 1920s, where he became a part of the \"Lost Generation,\" a group of expatriate writers. His experiences in Paris would later be the foundation for his novel \"A Moveable Feast.\" His first important work, \"The Sun Also Rises,\" was published in 1926 and established him as a leading writer of his generation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
