          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Literary Style and Influence</strong><br>\nHemingway was known for his distinctive writing style, marked by economy and understatement. He influenced 20th-century fiction with his theory of omission, where his prose stripped away as much as possible to leave the core of the story intact. This 'iceberg theory' allowed the underlying truths of his narratives to emerge more dramatically.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Adventures</strong><br>\nHemingway's personal life was as adventurous as his novels. He was married four times and lived in various locations including Paris, Key West, and Cuba. His adventurous spirit took him on many travels, including safaris in Africa, hunting in the American West, and fishing in the Caribbean, which provided material for his writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>War Correspondence</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Hemingway served as a correspondent and was present at several major war events, including the Normandy Landings and the liberation of Paris. His journalistic work influenced his fictional writing, bringing a sense of immediacy and realism to his depiction of war.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
