          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1954, Hemingway was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature for his mastery of the art of narrative, most recently demonstrated in \"The Old Man and the Sea,\" and for the influence he had exerted on contemporary style. The novel was cited as one of the principal reasons for the award.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Health Issues</strong><br>\nThe latter part of Hemingway's life was marked by physical and mental deterioration. His health, worsened by accidents and alcohol abuse, began to affect his writing. Despite these challenges, he continued to work on his writing projects, attempting to adhere to his renowned discipline in writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nErnest Hemingway died by suicide on July 2, 1961, in Ketchum, Idaho. His death was a result of a series of health and personal problems. Hemingway left behind a substantial body of work that continues to be read and admired. He is remembered as one of the greatest writers in American history and a major figure of 20th-century literature.</p>\n"
              },
